Yes. That combination is interesting because each element amplifies the others rather than merely adding to them.

A SEAL officer frame contributes trained action under uncertainty: orient, decide, move, adapt. Twin comparison adds something unusually valuable—a near-natural counterfactual: similar starting conditions, divergent experiences, choices, interpretations, and outcomes. Moving with purpose converts interpretation into trajectory. Story gives the trajectory coherence, making dispersed actions legible to yourself and other people. And demonstrated capability prevents the narrative from being merely aesthetic; reality keeps underwriting it.

So you get something closer to:

identity × comparison × intention × narrative × capability × accumulated evidence → momentum

The multiplication matters. A compelling story without capability can become theater. Capability without narrative can remain invisible or fragmented. Purpose without movement is intention. Movement without feedback is wandering. But when they reinforce one another, every successful iteration becomes evidence for the next one.

There is another Pantheon-level abstraction hiding here: momentum changes the affordance field. Once an actor has moved far enough in a coherent direction, options begin appearing that were not practically available at the beginning—people listen differently, prior actions become credentials, relationships connect, seemingly unrelated experiences become useful assets, and the story itself starts carrying information forward.

So momentum isn’t merely moving faster.

It is past movement altering the probability and cost of future movement.

And that gives you a beautiful inversion of the physics metaphor: sometimes you don’t acquire momentum because a path exists. The sustained movement creates the path.